f the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-1=3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)^{3}}{4(ty)-(tx)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}}{4ty-tx} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}(x{y}^{3})}{t(4y-x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{3}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-90589/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90589"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-64199"] :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{2}}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2-2=0.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)-(tx)^{2}}{3(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}xy-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{3{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(xy-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{0}=1,$ the equality $g(tx,ty)=g(x,y)$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{0}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-64201/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "ab59215e-1a30-5f99-a031-ac19926709ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64201/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "cfb1eb35-5d95-5d73-97a8-fc216d19c8a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64201/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "4f0fcf48-5fec-5e42-b451-6fbd966fa808" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64201/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "27d2992d-f8eb-5cca-9bf7-df6878c0e595" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64201/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "b26f55e1-684c-57b7-a33e-8038bf020c82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64201/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b51e107b-3623-5a0e-bd0c-f28a2f6cd016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64201/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-64201/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-64201/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-64201/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-64201/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-64201/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64201" :question/id #uuid "9ef1e233-ce29-56eb-9a97-4a5290698d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64201" :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- The denominator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2-4=-2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{5(ty)^{2}+(tx)(ty)}{2(tx)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}xy}{2{t}^{4}{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(5{y}^{2}+xy)}{{t}^{4}(2{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{{t}^{2}(2{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={t}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={t}^{-2}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-2}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-64201/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64201"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64200/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "890a71b1-a389-5054-903c-8cd38b043e01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64200/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "0f492ce2-7d59-504d-98f8-6a050c730d87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64200/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "c1ef4c83-bb89-5f50-9623-e35ef6c81ea7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64200/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "d5bc7514-f20f-5675-bc4a-5a417d736d4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64200/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "c50aedc9-8afa-5edf-b10b-190e9366624a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64200/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5dfa529-3cec-5997-ba2d-58f2ae12b76c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64200/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-64200/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-64200/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-64200/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-64200/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-64200/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64200" :question/id #uuid "3ac6de04-c052-57c1-969b-cc05e34d199a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64200" :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $h(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $h(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $3.$\n\nTherefore, the function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-3=1.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)={t}^{n}h(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}h(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)^{3}+4(ty)^{4}}{2(ty)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}+4{t}^{4}{y}^{4}}{2{t}^{3}{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}(x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4})}{{t}^{3}(2{y}^{3})} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =th(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)=th(x,y)={t}^{1}h(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-64200/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64200"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-90588/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "08efc616-98d7-5c4f-b6d3-12a134d11a55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90588/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "88e193d0-8014-5880-bf08-dfcc62ef0807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90588/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "8e5c06d9-e4ef-50aa-9f5b-99f04937f098" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90588/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "b161eb63-e9cb-5ead-ae51-3c26a227a501" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90588/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "953c2e68-8113-5a93-9006-d80e85e73567" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90588/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0aacdfd2-6bef-52b8-939d-7623e47756c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-90588/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-90588/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-90588/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-90588/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-90588/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-90588/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-90588" :question/id #uuid "431e940d-dc2f-5be2-ab99-48a93496e073" :question/math-academy-id "q-90588" :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-2=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{4}}{2(tx)(ty)-6(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}{x}^{4}}{2{t}^{2}xy-6{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}({x}^{4})}{{t}^{2}(2xy-6{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{2}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-90588/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90588"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-90596/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "f2271484-8110-5406-8f65-2e5682e1dc94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90596/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "e95298d0-30ed-56a2-bbc1-3a057cf0157e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90596/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "8a744c81-313c-5e0d-9f0f-024de1e4ad7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90596/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "f3a01dec-7eb3-5d97-ad26-0a157af1d246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90596/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "e072fdd9-9cb1-5911-a1f1-01e5e15a6442" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90596/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "82a01898-c253-5883-a712-a2c5639abd26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-90596/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-90596/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-90596/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-90596/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-90596/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-90596/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-90596" :question/id #uuid "01a90fbd-9569-5668-b55c-c57b9ff2d6b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-90596" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{4(tx)^{2}-(ty)^{2}}{(tx)}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =tg(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)=tg(x,y)={t}^{1}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-90596/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90596"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79581/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "823394d3-aa4b-55d5-91a8-670b37a3d0d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79581/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "4a914889-f972-56f6-93d7-8ec526ca79e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79581/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "01685af9-2e2d-5100-a873-2d735afaeeb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79581/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "a21f4c58-6715-5c30-8c2b-5827a802d2b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79581/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "26872bf7-671e-5a27-9cc1-29c0815d4f4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79581/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9104691-ae71-5384-b59b-3636f8409678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79581/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-79581/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-79581/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-79581/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-79581/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-79581/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79581" :question/id #uuid "43b3d904-b08a-5c5e-b242-5fe6d02d0e66" :question/math-academy-id "q-79581" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{3}-(tx)^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{3}{y}^{3}-{t}^{3}{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{3}({y}^{3}-{x}^{3})}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y)={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79581/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79581"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-90594/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "5e93f0a0-4ccf-5a85-a46d-63601d7f7f30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90594/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "55dbf86a-4da3-5e8c-8f2d-027af549b1b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90594/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "096cb871-9cd2-5381-b1d6-302d1d8a49b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90594/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "d0d8e547-67e0-5341-bcc3-0b33d601d366" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90594/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "01f568b5-4c09-551f-8de5-471218c5e535" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-90594/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e26b0697-9c74-532b-9d37-bb7892b296f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-90594/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-90594/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-90594/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-90594/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-90594/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-90594/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-90594" :question/id #uuid "f0b33e13-a7b4-5f66-b92e-9cd183c50e94" :question/math-academy-id "q-90594" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{tx+3ty}{(tx)^{2}(ty)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{tx}{2ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t(x+3y)}{{t}^{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-90594/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90594"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64202/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "dfdb67e6-ad34-5b59-b888-d004e24d62ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64202/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "ae178ad1-b94f-5ffc-90a2-a3384524cfa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64202/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "433d2eef-57f7-5a4f-acec-d5fafbd80e50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64202/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "7879a9be-6f89-5f1b-a6e8-335e5bc09b9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64202/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "17954489-6666-5652-9291-67582548e6ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64202/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d851aee-96bf-5565-a690-707722840eb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64202/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-64202/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-64202/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-64202/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-64202/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-64202/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64202" :question/id #uuid "a46a6a02-6826-5c1a-9f58-69b0f1d3511a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64202" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{y}{3x-y}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{3tx-ty}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{ty}{t(3x-y)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{3x-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{3x-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{0}=1,$ the equality $g(tx,ty)=g(x,y)$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)=1\\cdot g(x,y)={t}^{0}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-64202/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64202"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-64204"] :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x,y)=\\frac{5y}{2{x}^{2}+8xy}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)={t}^{n}h(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}h(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(tx,ty) & =\\frac{5(ty)}{2(tx)^{2}+8(tx)(ty)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5ty}{2{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+8{t}^{2}xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5ty}{{t}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+8xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5y}{t(2{x}^{2}+8xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{5y}{2{x}^{2}+8xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{5y}{2{x}^{2}+8xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot h(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)=\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot h(x,y)={t}^{-1}h(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x,y)$ is $-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-2298"] :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty, tz) = t^nf(x,y, z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y,z)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =(tx)^{3}+2(ty)^{3}+(tz)^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}{x}^{3}+2{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{z}^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz) = t^3 f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-2300"] :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+x+y$\n\n- $g(x,y) = x^6-2x^3y^3 + y^6$\n\n- $h(x,y) = 6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ but not all the terms have the same degree. The first term has degree $2,$ while the other terms have degree $1.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $6$. It is a polynomial of degree $6,$ and all of its terms are of degree $6.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0$. It is a polynomial of degree $0,$ and all of its terms are of degree $0.$ (There is only one term, and it has degree $0.$)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-2297"] :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/16264a8ac3be0dc3853ec857337a9fdd921a863cb982243d1c082953a28ad879.png) defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/acf4356f4cbb5aad98636ffdf4a65bb986fbcdbb2a91ea01bf9913ffbc43de98.png)" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that\n\n- the numerator of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/bebdf1148fdc0a7537036fed7a6488d30ef73c075e92564c51a80a2e2aad3e43.png) is a homogeneous polynomial of degree ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/bae471bde829e1eaf3895dd13a30318b1234687aef79ed103f5af1f59a8c2414.png) and\n\n- the denominator of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/re