57b287d9c0cb5eec4b825014b42dfb37092d6592d225299b1096ebe08bb5.png):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/acead36aa36a5f642d2b5d408f390634be3d01274abfb6a4196d8d332a0f82d5.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/b7a2044031e99d2071771a1c3d1f71d6a4fd07a57b283c081d40939525b73254.png)\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/47070dbaf25d08b50c3f42c5f4ef9ce7d02b4e541112fa3049126f2d5cdcffed.png) is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/bf820cdf3770e08974a14218498296ee4390accf8a18601af55422206ce41385.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-2299"] :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
